1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
A hydrocarbon question can ask you to name

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:05,600
a molecule, sort its isomers, or predict what

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:06,320
it makes.

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,480
Each one here comes down to three checks.

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:11,120
Which bonds does it have?

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,640
What's added or swapped, and under what conditions?

7
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,280
And do the atoms and the name check

8
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:17,960
out?

9
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:21,080
Here are four HSC-style questions.

10
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:23,520
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

11
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,440
it through, one step at a time.

12
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:27,786
Question one.

13
00:00:28,107 --> 00:00:31,466
Two hydrocarbons, A and B, are shown as

14
00:00:31,507 --> 00:00:32,986
skeletal formulas.

15
00:00:33,307 --> 00:00:36,026
Give the IUPAC name of each compound.

16
00:00:36,347 --> 00:00:39,626
Then identify the type of structural isomerism shown

17
00:00:39,747 --> 00:00:42,666
by A and B, and justify your answer.

18
00:00:42,827 --> 00:00:44,026
Pause here to answer.

19
00:00:54,434 --> 00:00:55,473
Start with A.

20
00:00:55,874 --> 00:00:58,513
Its longest chain has six carbons and one

21
00:00:58,594 --> 00:01:00,753
double bond, so it's a hexene.

22
00:01:01,314 --> 00:01:03,633
Number from the end nearer the double bond,

23
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:07,433
and it starts at carbon two: hex-two-ene.

24
00:01:07,874 --> 00:01:08,753
Now B.

25
00:01:08,994 --> 00:01:11,233
Its longest chain has five carbons, with a

26
00:01:11,354 --> 00:01:12,513
methyl branch.

27
00:01:12,594 --> 00:01:14,913
The double bond wins the numbering, so count

28
00:01:14,994 --> 00:01:16,433
from the right-hand end.

29
00:01:16,594 --> 00:01:18,793
That puts the double bond at carbon two,

30
00:01:18,834 --> 00:01:23,233
and the methyl on carbon four: four-methylpent-two-ene.

31
00:01:24,434 --> 00:01:25,953
Now count the atoms.

32
00:01:26,194 --> 00:01:29,793
Each is C six H twelve: six carbons,

33
00:01:29,874 --> 00:01:32,993
and one double bond, so two fewer hydrogens

34
00:01:33,074 --> 00:01:33,873
than the alkane.

35
00:01:34,354 --> 00:01:38,673
Same formula, different structures, so they're structural isomers.

36
00:01:38,914 --> 00:01:41,793
Both are alkenes, so it's not functional group

37
00:01:41,874 --> 00:01:42,833
isomerism.

38
00:01:43,074 --> 00:01:46,033
What changed is the carbon skeleton: A is

39
00:01:46,074 --> 00:01:48,753
a straight chain, and B is branched.

40
00:01:48,834 --> 00:01:50,513
So they're chain isomers.

41
00:01:51,734 --> 00:01:54,213
The classic mistake is numbering B from the

42
00:01:54,294 --> 00:01:56,053
left, the way you read it.

43
00:01:56,294 --> 00:01:59,053
That gives two-methylpent-three-ene.

44
00:01:59,334 --> 00:02:01,253
The branch gets the low number, but the

45
00:02:01,334 --> 00:02:03,573
double bond is named in the ending, so

46
00:02:03,734 --> 00:02:05,973
it gets first claim on the low numbers.

47
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:08,000
Question two.

48
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:12,400
Pent-two-ene reacts with hydrogen chloride at room temperature.

49
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:16,240
In a separate reaction, pentane reacts with chlorine

50
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:17,520
under UV light.

51
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:21,520
For each reaction, identify the type of reaction,

52
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,040
and write a balanced equation, using structural formulas,

53
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:27,920
for the formation of one organic product.

54
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:29,680
Pause here to answer.

55
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:41,860
Start with pent-two-ene.

56
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:44,820
It has a double bond, and hydrogen chloride

57
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:47,700
needs no light or catalyst, so this is

58
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:50,340
addition: the H goes on one carbon of

59
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:52,260
the double bond, and the Cl on the

60
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:52,660
other.

61
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:55,940
Pent-two-ene isn't the same at both ends, so

62
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:57,860
there are two possible products.

63
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:00,180
Put the Cl on carbon two, and you

64
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:02,020
get two-chloropentane.

65
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:04,580
Three-chloropentane is just as correct.

66
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:08,020
Either way, two molecules make one, and nothing

67
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:08,740
is lost.

68
00:03:09,967 --> 00:03:11,086
Now pentane.

69
00:03:11,407 --> 00:03:13,886
It has only single bonds, so it can't

70
00:03:13,967 --> 00:03:14,366
add.

71
00:03:14,847 --> 00:03:17,966
The UV light gives the energy for substitution:

72
00:03:18,047 --> 00:03:20,526
one hydrogen swaps for a chlorine, and that

73
00:03:20,607 --> 00:03:22,606
hydrogen leaves as H Cl.

74
00:03:23,087 --> 00:03:25,086
Swap one on an end carbon, and you

75
00:03:25,207 --> 00:03:27,166
get one-chloropentane.

76
00:03:27,487 --> 00:03:30,926
Notice that both reactions make a chloropentane, C

77
00:03:31,007 --> 00:03:32,846
five H eleven Cl.

78
00:03:33,327 --> 00:03:35,806
What tells them apart is the starting bonds,

79
00:03:35,887 --> 00:03:37,406
and the H Cl.

80
00:03:38,634 --> 00:03:41,353
The classic mistake is treating pentane like an

81
00:03:41,514 --> 00:03:44,313
alkene, and putting a chlorine on two neighbouring

82
00:03:44,394 --> 00:03:45,233
carbons.

83
00:03:45,514 --> 00:03:48,713
Count the atoms: that product is two hydrogens

84
00:03:48,794 --> 00:03:51,193
short, so the equation can't balance.

85
00:03:51,674 --> 00:03:54,313
An alkane has no double bond to open.

86
00:03:55,534 --> 00:03:56,693
Question three.

87
00:03:56,974 --> 00:04:00,813
A hydrocarbon, X, reacts with excess chlorine at

88
00:04:00,894 --> 00:04:02,933
room temperature, in the dark.

89
00:04:03,214 --> 00:04:06,373
The only organic product is one, one, two,

90
00:04:06,414 --> 00:04:08,533
two-tetrachlorobutane.

91
00:04:08,814 --> 00:04:12,173
Identify X, and explain your reasoning, including the

92
00:04:12,254 --> 00:04:13,493
type of reaction.

93
00:04:13,774 --> 00:04:15,213
Pause here to answer.

94
00:04:25,634 --> 00:04:27,673
Work backwards from the product.

95
00:04:27,954 --> 00:04:30,353
It's in the dark, with no UV light,

96
00:04:30,434 --> 00:04:32,193
so this isn't substitution.

97
00:04:32,674 --> 00:04:33,873
Chlorine has added.

98
00:04:34,434 --> 00:04:37,313
The product has four chlorine atoms, so two

99
00:04:37,394 --> 00:04:40,193
chlorine molecules added to each X molecule.

100
00:04:40,834 --> 00:04:43,553
A double bond can open only once, so

101
00:04:43,634 --> 00:04:45,553
it would add one chlorine molecule.

102
00:04:46,114 --> 00:04:49,313
Adding twice needs two extra bonds: a triple

103
00:04:49,394 --> 00:04:51,633
bond, or two double bonds.

104
00:04:52,834 --> 00:04:53,553
Now where.

105
00:04:53,634 --> 00:04:56,593
All four chlorines are on carbons one and

106
00:04:56,674 --> 00:04:58,113
two, two on each.

107
00:04:58,594 --> 00:05:01,073
So both additions happened across the same pair

108
00:05:01,114 --> 00:05:01,953
of carbons.

109
00:05:02,434 --> 00:05:04,913
That needs a triple bond, between carbon one

110
00:05:05,074 --> 00:05:06,033
and carbon two.

111
00:05:06,674 --> 00:05:08,593
Take the chlorines off, and put the triple

112
00:05:08,674 --> 00:05:09,633
bond back.

113
00:05:09,954 --> 00:05:14,113
X is but-one-yne, C H triple bond C,

114
00:05:14,194 --> 00:05:16,513
C H two, C H three.

115
00:05:16,594 --> 00:05:19,873
Check: C four H six, plus two Cl

116
00:05:20,034 --> 00:05:23,073
two, gives C four H six Cl four.

117
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:27,180
The classic mistake is but-one-ene.

118
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:29,500
Its double bond is in the right place,

119
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:32,060
but it can add only one chlorine molecule,

120
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:36,780
giving one, two-dichlorobutane, with just two chlorines.

121
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:38,960
Question four.

122
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:42,880
X is an open-chain hydrocarbon with one multiple

123
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:43,360
bond.

124
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:47,120
Its molecular formula is C five H eight,

125
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:50,400
and it decolourises bromine water at room temperature,

126
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:51,680
without UV light.

127
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:55,120
A student concludes that X is an alkene,

128
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:57,280
because it decolourises bromine water.

129
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:59,600
Evaluate the student's conclusion.

130
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:01,520
Pause here to answer.

131
00:06:11,934 --> 00:06:14,173
Start with what the test shows.

132
00:06:14,494 --> 00:06:17,533
Losing the orange colour means bromine has added,

133
00:06:17,614 --> 00:06:19,533
so X has a double bond or a

134
00:06:19,614 --> 00:06:20,333
triple bond.

135
00:06:20,814 --> 00:06:22,493
That rules out an alkane.

136
00:06:22,814 --> 00:06:26,893
But alkenes and alkynes both decolourise bromine water,

137
00:06:26,974 --> 00:06:29,413
so the test alone can't tell you which.

138
00:06:29,694 --> 00:06:31,773
The student has read more into the result

139
00:06:31,854 --> 00:06:32,813
than it can show.

140
00:06:34,034 --> 00:06:35,313
So use the formula.

141
00:06:35,634 --> 00:06:38,353
With five carbons, an alkene would be C

142
00:06:38,434 --> 00:06:41,553
n H two n, C five H ten.

143
00:06:42,034 --> 00:06:44,593
An alkyne is C n H two n

144
00:06:44,674 --> 00:06:48,033
minus two, C five H eight, which matches

145
00:06:48,194 --> 00:06:48,593
X.

146
00:06:49,074 --> 00:06:51,593
So the student's conclusion is incorrect.

147
00:06:51,794 --> 00:06:54,753
The test shows that X is unsaturated, and

148
00:06:54,834 --> 00:06:57,153
the formula shows it's an alkyne, such as

149
00:06:57,314 --> 00:06:58,593
pent-one-yne.

150
00:06:58,634 --> 00:07:01,233
It would add two bromine molecules, not one.

151
00:07:02,434 --> 00:07:05,153
The classic mistake is treating bromine water as

152
00:07:05,194 --> 00:07:07,633
a test for double bonds only.

153
00:07:07,674 --> 00:07:10,673
It's a test for unsaturation: a double or

154
00:07:10,754 --> 00:07:11,713
a triple bond.

155
00:07:12,934 --> 00:07:15,813
So here's how to start any hydrocarbon question.

156
00:07:16,294 --> 00:07:19,813
First, find the bonds: trace the longest chain

157
00:07:19,894 --> 00:07:21,973
with the multiple bond, and number from its

158
00:07:22,134 --> 00:07:23,133
nearer end.

159
00:07:23,414 --> 00:07:25,973
An alkane with a halogen and UV light

160
00:07:26,134 --> 00:07:29,813
means substitution: swap one hydrogen, and add H

161
00:07:29,894 --> 00:07:30,293
X.

162
00:07:30,774 --> 00:07:33,653
A double or triple bond means addition, one

163
00:07:33,734 --> 00:07:35,893
piece on each carbon, and a triple bond

164
00:07:35,974 --> 00:07:36,853
adds twice.

165
00:07:37,334 --> 00:07:39,653
Then count the atoms, and check the formula

166
00:07:39,814 --> 00:07:41,013
fits the family.

167
00:07:41,334 --> 00:07:44,133
Next, alcohols, and what an O H group

168
00:07:44,214 --> 00:07:45,413
does to a molecule.

169
00:07:46,634 --> 00:07:48,073
Here are the key notes.

170
00:07:48,314 --> 00:07:50,233
Pause the video if you need more time

171
00:07:50,314 --> 00:07:51,113
to copy them.
